new file mode 100644
index 00000000..4d975188
--- /dev/null
+++ b/kdesu/DESIGN.md
@@ -0,0 +1,106 @@
+# KDE su dialog
+
+Production phone approval uses the [protected execution interface](AUTHENTICATION.md)
+with a separate phone result and password fallback. The broker executes the approved
+command; the UI does not convert a phone response into password authentication.
+
+The dialog leads with the requesting process's name and icon together. The target
+program has a smaller name and icon beside the command heading, followed by the
+complete command and the signed-in user's account card. The password label names
+the account whose password the existing KDESu backend actually requires. That
+account can differ from both the signed-in user and the command's target account.
+
+The program name and icon come from the desktop entry when available. An explicit
+executable path that differs from the desktop executable retains its actual path
+and a generic icon. The existing `-i` option overrides only the target program
+icon; it cannot replace the requester's header icon. Codex has a capitalized name
+and a bundled, attributed vector fallback when no desktop entry or theme icon is
+installed. Installed Codex theme icons take precedence over the bundled artwork.
+The requester is the immediate parent process, obtained from `/proc`; its full
+path and PID are available in the details and to assistive technology. This is
+process metadata, not a verified application identity.
+
+## Command presentation
+
+- The large monospace field is read-only, selectable, copyable, and wraps long
+  commands. The displayed command is never rewritten for execution.
+- `-d` suppresses the command, its breakdown, and command-derived application
+  metadata. A short explanation makes the omission visible.
+- The expandable breakdown uses `KShell::splitArgs` with `AbortOnMeta`. Quoted
+  arguments remain grouped and are rendered as text, including HTML-like text.
+- Shell expressions, substitutions, assignments, keywords, and unmatched quotes
+  get an explanation of the parsing limit instead of a speculative breakdown.
+  Nothing is expanded or executed to generate the explanation. Argument syntax
+  alone cannot establish what an arbitrary program will do.
+
+## Accessibility and interaction
+
+- Native Qt/KDE controls expose their roles, names, values, and keyboard actions.
+  The password's accessible name identifies its authentication account, and its
+  accessible state remains a protected password field.
+- Initial focus goes to the password. Mnemonics reach the command, details,
+  password, remember-password checkbox, authentication, and unprivileged action.
+  Enter authenticates from the password field; Escape cancels. Static labels do
+  not add unnecessary stops to Tab navigation.
+- Focused controls are scrolled into view. Content can scroll on small displays
+  or with enlarged text; the action buttons stay outside that scrolling area and
+  stack vertically when necessary. Details expand the dialog within screen bounds.
+- Font sizes derive from the desktop font. Commands retain left-to-right
+  presentation inside a mirrored right-to-left interface.
+- Colors follow the desktop palette. The primary button uses whichever of black
+  or white gives sufficient text contrast against the accent. Keyboard focus has
+  a visible border, and information is not conveyed by color alone.
+- Native password reveal behavior respects `lineedit_reveal_password`. Caps Lock
+  feedback uses KGuiAddons on platforms that provide the modifier state.
+- Errors remain in the dialog and raise an accessibility alert. Failed attempts
+  clear the password and return focus. Fatal errors disable authentication while
+  retaining cancellation. A queued attempt cannot accept after cancellation.
+- There are no decorative animations, remote explanations, or new credential
+  storage. Password retention continues to use the existing kdesu behavior.
+
+Automated checks cover requester/target separation, Codex metadata and icon
+fallbacks, executable path mismatches, parsing boundaries, literal rendering, command privacy,
+account labels, accessibility properties, keyboard submission, retry, cancellation,
+fatal errors, the Ignore result, and focus visibility on a narrow window. Native
+Plasma renders cover light/dark palettes, doubled text size, long commands, narrow
+windows, and right-to-left layout. Actual screen-reader user testing and real PAM
+authentication are still separate validation steps; mocked tests do not prove them.
+
+## Development preview
+
+From the `kde-cli-tools` source directory:
+
+```sh
+cmake -S . -B ../build/kde-cli-tools -DBUILD_DOC=OFF -DBUILD_TESTING=ON -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
+cmake --build ../build/kde-cli-tools --target kdesu_executable kdesu_dialog_test kdesu_dialog_preview -j8
+ctest --test-dir ../build/kde-cli-tools -R '^kdesu_dialog_test$' --output-on-failure
+../build/kde-cli-tools/bin/kdesu_dialog_preview
+```
+
+The preview is a separate, uninstalled executable. It never checks a password or
+runs the command. It supports `--command`, `--expanded`, `--hidden`, `--rtl`,
+`--font-scale 2`, `--width`, `--height`, `--palette BreezeLight`,
+`--palette BreezeDark`, and `--screenshot /path/to/image.png`. The explicit
+`--notify-phone` switch also exercises the [phone notification interface](NOTIFICATIONS.md)
+with `source: "kdesu-preview"`; it still cannot authenticate or execute commands.
+
+The dialog adds a direct KGuiAddons dependency for Caps Lock state. A Gentoo build
+must include `kde-frameworks/kguiaddons` in the `kdesu-gui` dependencies and apply
+the saved package preparation separately from this development checkout. The
+historical preview notification transport also links Qt Network for its local socket.
+Production phone authentication uses QProcess private pipes to the protected
+optional helper, with no listening socket or daemon.
+
+## Fingerprint authentication
+
+At inspection on 2026-09-06, this machine had no installed fprintd package and its
+`su`/`system-auth` PAM configuration had no fingerprint module. KDESu's current
+`SuProcess::converseSU()` implements a password prompt conversation, not a
+general PAM challenge interface.
+
+A working fingerprint option therefore needs supported/enrolled hardware, an
+administrator-configured authentication path, and a backend that handles its
+challenge, retry, cancellation, and fallback states. A fingerprint match reported
+to the dialog alone is not authority to execute a privileged command. The current
+implementation does not advertise a nonfunctional fingerprint button or change
+the system's PAM configuration.
