index 00000000..1fa55ee1
--- /dev/null
+++ b/kdesu/NOTIFICATIONS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,145 @@
+# Phone notification interface, version 1
+
+This interface mirrors the lifetime of a visible kdesu prompt to the paired-phone
+host bridge. It carries display context only. It neither checks phone signatures
+nor accepts authentication results; kdesu still authenticates and executes through
+its existing KDESu/su path. Inbound socket data is discarded.
+
+## Transport
+
+The host listens on an AF_UNIX stream socket at
+`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/navi-auth/events.sock`. It owns the containing directory with
+mode 0700 and the socket with mode 0600. The client rejects symlinks at either of
+these two paths, checks ownership/private permissions, and checks the server's
+`SO_PEERCRED` UID against its real UID on Linux. The host must also check the
+client's peer credentials; JSON fields are not credentials. Other platforms
+currently do not send events.
+
+Each dialog opens one connection and emits UTF-8 JSON lines. The limit is 65536
+bytes per line including the newline. Embedded newlines in strings are JSON
+escaped. An oversized start is omitted rather than truncating the command. The
+client connects asynchronously, abandons an incomplete connection after 1500 ms,
+and does not reconnect, replay, or wait for a response. An absent bridge does not
+affect desktop password authentication.
+
+The host must treat EOF/disconnection as withdrawal of every pending request for
+that connection, including when no cancel line arrived. Destruction and process
+death close the connection; cancel delivery alone is not a sufficient lifetime
+guarantee. A disconnected or cancelled request must never be accepted, replayed,
+or revived by a late phone signature.
+
+## Start
+
+The first show of the dialog emits one start event:
+
+```json
+{
+  "protocol": "navi-auth-desktop/v1",
+  "event": "start",
+  "request_id": "085e5872-32ad-4e08-baf7-17f86e88576d",
+  "mode": "notification-only",
+  "context": {
+    "application": "Codex",
+    "action_id": "org.kde.kdesu.run",
+    "command": "systemsettings kcm_users",
+    "command_hidden": false,
+    "requesting_user": "alice",
+    "requesting_uid": 1000,
+    "target_user": "root",
+    "source": "kdesu",
+    "requester_pid": 123,
+    "requester_executable": "/path/to/codex",
+    "icon_name": "codex",
+    "icon_png_base64": "<standard base64 PNG>"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`request_id` is a fresh UUID scoped to this connection and dialog. The host issues
+its own cryptographic request ID, nonce, expiry, and machine identity, associating
+them with this desktop ID. The desktop does not supply a machine name, key, or
+certificate. The phone should prominently display the machine name bound to the
+paired machine identity; the host must authenticate that identity and sign the
+complete phone payload, including the icon and notification-only status. This
+notification-only phase must be identified as such in the phone UI.
+
+The application name and image are resolved from the same immediate parent
+executable as the desktop header. A target's `-i` override cannot affect these
+fields. This process metadata is descriptive, not a verified application identity.
+The context contains no password or password-retention state.
+
+`icon_name` is a desktop theme identifier or an empty string. Optional
+`icon_png_base64` decodes to a PNG no larger than 16384 bytes and 256 by 256 pixels.
+The current encoder starts at 128 physical pixels and reduces resolution to meet
+the byte budget; an unencodable image is omitted. The phone must validate these
+bounds and decode only after verifying the signed payload. No remote icon URLs or
+local icon paths are transmitted.
+
+With `-d`, `command` is empty and `command_hidden` is true. The host must preserve
+that omission rather than substituting command text. A normal request carries the
+exact displayed command, without executing or rewriting it.
+
+The initial Android bridge applies stricter display limits than this transport:
+application names over 80 characters, commands over 512 characters, and commands
+containing newlines or tabs receive no phone notification. The bridge logs the
+rejection; neither the command nor desktop authentication is altered. Increasing
+these limits requires coordinated changes to the broker, host, and Android app.
+The bridge currently handles one active request and rejects additional dialogs.
+
+## Withdrawal
+
+```json
+{
+  "protocol": "navi-auth-desktop/v1",
+  "event": "cancel",
+  "request_id": "085e5872-32ad-4e08-baf7-17f86e88576d",
+  "reason": "dismissed"
+}
+```
+
+| Reason | Trigger |
+| --- | --- |
+| `dismissed` | Cancel, Escape, or closing the dialog |
+| `authenticated` | Successful password check closes the dialog |
+| `run-as-user` | Run as me, without elevated privileges |
+| `unavailable` | Fatal authentication error or transport failure |
+| `closed` | Destruction without an earlier withdrawal |
+
+Withdrawal is idempotent and closes the connection. A failed, retryable password
+attempt keeps the notification alive. Hiding and showing the same dialog does not
+issue another start. The host imposes its normal expiry (currently 120 seconds);
+this client does not renew expired phone requests.
+
+The host invalidates the pending request before withdrawing the phone notification.
+A late response must not win a race with cancellation. Neither `authenticated`
+nor any other cancellation reason means that the command was actually executed.
+
+## Development and authority boundary
+
+The current production kdesu instead uses the separate
+[protected execution interface](AUTHENTICATION.md). This notification-only
+transport remains available to explicit previews. Tests and normal previews do
+not connect it. For an
+explicit integration exercise against a running host bridge:
+
+```sh
+../build/kde-cli-tools/bin/kdesu_dialog_preview --notify-phone
+```
+
+The preview uses `source: "kdesu-preview"`. It never validates a password or runs
+the displayed command. Closing it exercises withdrawal on the same connection.
+
+Real phone-backed elevation needs a privileged PAM integration into the su
+authentication path, or a defined Polkit broker/action that verifies the challenge
+and executes the exact authorized operation. The existing `checkInstall(password)`
+and later `SuProcess::exec(password)` are separate password-based interactions.
+Changing the dialog result from a phone approval does not convey authority to su.
+No PAM, Polkit, system package, or phone configuration is changed by this hook.
+Only prompts from the rebuilt kdesu use this hook; other desktop authentication
+agents, including Polkit agents, need their own integration.
+
+## Historical status
+
+The notification-only preview was exercised during development. It cannot
+authorize execution. Use AUTHENTICATION.md and this repository's public docs
+for the deployed private-pipe authority and current verification scope.
